Post 2 - Linux L2/L3 Interview Questions — Answers & Explanations

Q4. You are unable to unmount a filesystem. What could be the possible reasons?
The most common error is:
device is busy
Possible reasons
1. A process has an open file
Check:
lsof +D /mountpoint
or:
fuser -vm /mountpoint
2. A user’s current directory is inside the filesystem
For example:
cd /data
umount /data
The shell itself may be using /data.
Move out:
cd /
Then:
umount /data
3. A process is executing something from that filesystem
For example:
/data/application/app
is currently running.
4. Nested mount exists
Check:
findmnt /data
or:
mount | grep /data
5. NFS filesystem is stale/unresponsive
NFS can remain busy if the server is unavailable.
6. Swap file is located there
A filesystem containing an active swap file cannot simply be unmounted.
Useful troubleshooting
fuser -vm /data
lsof +D /data
findmnt /data
Linux cannot unmount a filesystem while it is busy, such as when processes have open files or working directories there. 
Last resort
umount -l /data
This is a lazy unmount. Don’t use it blindly on production systems. 
Q5. What could be the reason if a server takes a long time to come up after a reboot?
There can be many reasons.
Common causes
1. Incorrect /etc/fstab entry
2. Missing disk/device
3. NFS mount unavailable
4. DNS/network problems
5. Filesystem check (fsck) taking a long time
6. A systemd service hanging
7. Hardware/RAID/storage problems
8. Network interface timeout
9. Bootloader/initramfs issue
10. Dependency/service timeout
Troubleshooting commands
Check systemd boot time:
systemd-analyze
Check slow services:
systemd-analyze blame
Check dependency chain:
systemd-analyze critical-chain
Check previous boot logs:
journalctl -b -1
Check kernel messages:
dmesg
Check mounts:
findmnt
Check /etc/fstab:
cat /etc/fstab
scenario
If the server takes exactly 90 seconds during boot, I would especially investigate a missing/unreachable device or network mount because systemd may be waiting for a timeout.

Q6. You are unable to create new files in a partition even though there is enough disk space and you have the required permissions. What could be the possible reasons?

The first thing I would check is inode availability.
1. Inodes exhausted
Run:
df -i
You might see:
Filesystem      Inodes  IUsed  IFree IUse%
/dev/sdb1       1000000 1000000 0    100%
Even if:
df -h
shows plenty of free GB, you cannot create files because there are no free inodes. 
2. Filesystem mounted read-only
Check:
mount | grep /mountpoint
or:
findmnt /mountpoint
Look for:
ro
Try:
touch /mountpoint/test
You may get:
Read-only file system 3. Disk quota exceeded
Check:
quota -u username
or filesystem-specific quota tools. 4. Filesystem-specific limits
Certain filesystem structures can become exhausted even when apparent disk space remains.
5. Reserved blocks
On ext filesystems, some blocks may be reserved for privileged/system use.
Check:
tune2fs -l /dev/sdb1 | grep -i reserved